        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-glass" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why People Want a Glass Filter Pitcher
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Complaints About Plastic Jugs</h3>

          <p>
            Most people who search for a glass water filter pitcher have already owned a plastic one. The complaints are consistent: the jug picks up a faint taste after a few months, it scratches and goes cloudy, it stains if you ever put anything but water in it, and it sits in the fridge looking like a piece of lab equipment. Glass fixes all four. It does not hold odors, it does not scratch the way polycarbonate and Tritan do, it can go in a dishwasher without warping, and it looks fine on a dinner table.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Less Plastic Contact</h3>

          <p>
            The second reason is plastic contact. A filtered water pitcher holds the water you drink for hours or days at a time, often cold, sometimes in a warm kitchen. People who have read about microplastics and plastic additives would rather that water sit in an inert material. Glass is about as inert as food contact surfaces get. That is a reasonable preference, and it is the main reason glass pitchers cost two to three times what a basic plastic pitcher costs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Taste, the Weakest Reason</h3>

          <p>
            The third reason is taste, and it is the weakest of the three. Glass does not add flavor, but neither does a clean, new plastic pitcher. Most of the taste improvement people notice comes from the carbon in the filter removing chlorine, not from the jug. If your water tastes bad, a glass pitcher with a weak filter will still taste bad. The filter does the work; the glass decides what the water sits in afterward.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Honest Framing</h3>

          <p>
            So the honest framing is this: a glass pitcher is a plastic-reduction and durability-of-appearance choice, and the filter inside it decides whether it actually cleans your water. The rest of this guide is about choosing the filter as carefully as the glass. If you have not yet settled whether a pitcher is the right tool at all, our main <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">water filter pitcher guide</a> compares the whole category, including the cheaper plastic models.
          </p>

          <p>
            Glass pitchers are also a narrower market than plastic ones. A handful of brands make them for the US market, and only a few of those publish any third-party certification for the filter. That shrinks the field quickly once you insist on documented claims, which is why this list is five products rather than ten.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plastic-caveat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Honest Caveat: The Filter Is Still Plastic
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Parts Are Still Plastic</h3>

          <p>
            Here is the part most glass pitcher listings leave out. On almost every glass filter pitcher sold in the US, the filter cartridge is a plastic shell, the reservoir that holds unfiltered water above the filter is plastic, and the lid is plastic. The water passes through plastic on its way into the glass. What the glass changes is where the filtered water sits afterward, which is where it spends most of its time.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is still a meaningful reduction. Filtered water in a plastic pitcher spends hours or days in contact with plastic walls. In a glass pitcher it spends a few minutes passing through a plastic cartridge and then sits in glass. If your concern is long contact time between drinking water and plastic, glass cuts most of it. If your concern is any plastic contact at all, no gravity pitcher on the US market fully solves it.
          </p>

