          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Aarke Exception</h3>

          <p>
            The Aarke Purifier is the closest thing to an exception. Its cartridge is stainless steel and you refill it with loose granules, so the cartridge shell in the water path is metal. Aarke still lists food-grade thermoplastic and silicone among its materials, so it is less plastic rather than plastic-free. The catch, covered below, is that Aarke publishes no NSF/ANSI contaminant-reduction certification.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Be Wary of Plastic-Free Claims</h3>

          <p>
            Be suspicious of any listing that calls a filter pitcher completely plastic-free without saying what the filter is made of. Ask what the cartridge housing is, what the upper reservoir is, and what the lid is. If the answers are glass, stainless steel and silicone, the claim may hold. If the listing will not say, assume plastic.
          </p>

