        <div id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. NSF/ANSI 42, 53, 401 and 473 Decoded
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pitcher certifications are numbered standards, and each covers a different job. The numbers on a box mean nothing unless you know which contaminants were actually tested and which body certified them. The major certifiers are NSF, the Water Quality Association (WQA) and IAPMO, and each publishes a searchable database. A maker that says a filter was tested to a standard is making a weaker claim than one that says it was certified to it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Standard</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it covers</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why it matters for a pitcher</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NSF/ANSI 42</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Aesthetic effects: chlorine, taste, odor, particulates</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The baseline. Almost every pitcher has it.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NSF/ANSI 53</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Health effects: lead, mercury, cadmium, cysts, some VOCs, PFOA/PFOS</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only counts for the contaminants actually listed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NSF/ANSI 401</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Emerging contaminants: some pharmaceuticals, microplastics</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Relevant if microplastics are part of the reason you want glass</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NSF/ANSI 473</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">PFOA and PFOS (now folded into 53)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Older PFAS claim; look for the specific compounds</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NSF/ANSI 372</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lead content of the product's materials</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Says the parts are low-lead, not that the filter removes lead</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">NSF/ANSI 42: The Floor</h3>

          <p>
            NSF/ANSI 42 is the floor. It certifies that the filter reduces chlorine taste and odor and some particles. That is the job most people actually need on city water, and every pitcher in this guide either has it or claims an equivalent. It is not a health claim.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">NSF/ANSI 53: Where Health Claims Live</h3>

          <p>
            NSF/ANSI 53 is where the health claims live, and it is contaminant-specific. A pitcher certified to 53 for mercury is not certified for lead unless lead is on the list. That distinction separates the LifeStraw from the Waterdrop models here. LifeStraw lists WQA certification to 53 for lead, mercury, cadmium, copper, cysts and turbidity. Waterdrop's glass pitcher listings cite 53 alongside reductions of PFOA/PFOS, cadmium, copper and mercury, with no lead on the list.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">NSF/ANSI 401: Emerging Contaminants</h3>

          <p>
            NSF/ANSI 401 covers a set of emerging contaminants, including some pharmaceuticals and microplastics. It matters here because many glass buyers are motivated by plastics in the first place. LifeStraw lists WQA certification to 401 for microplastics. NSF P473, the older PFOA and PFOS protocol, has largely moved into Standard 53, so a newer pitcher may list its PFAS claim under 53 instead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">NSF/ANSI 372: Materials, Not Removal</h3>

          <p>
            NSF/ANSI 372 is the one that confuses people. It certifies that the materials of the product, the parts water touches, meet a low-lead content limit. It says nothing about whether the filter removes lead from your water. When a listing puts 372 next to 42 and 53, read the contaminant list for 53, not the number 372. We only state certifications that the makers themselves publish; we have not re-run their tests. If a claim matters to your health decision, look the exact model up in the certifier's database before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-breakage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Weight, Breakage and the Fridge Door
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Much a Full Pitcher Weighs</h3>

          <p>
            Glass is heavier than plastic, and a filter pitcher is mostly carried when full. Water weighs about 8.3 pounds per gallon, so a 10-cup pitcher holds roughly 5.2 pounds of water and a 7-cup pitcher about 3.6 pounds, before you add the jug. LifeStraw lists its glass Home pitcher at about 2 pounds 4 ounces empty and Aarke lists the standard Purifier at about 1.1 kilograms, or about 2.4 pounds. Expect a full glass pitcher in the 6 to 8 pound range.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Fridge Door Shelf</h3>

          <p>
            That matters in two places. The first is the refrigerator door shelf. Door bins are often molded plastic clips rated for a few bottles of condiments and a carton of milk. A full 10-cup glass pitcher is one of the heaviest things you can put there, and it swings every time the door opens. If your shelf already flexes under a gallon of milk, put the pitcher on a main shelf instead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Breakage and Choosing a Size</h3>

          <p>
            The second is breakage. Borosilicate glass, which LifeStraw specifies, handles temperature changes well, so cold water from a hot dishwasher is not a problem. It is not shatterproof. A drop onto tile will usually break it. LifeStraw's silicone base protects the bottom edge from knocks on the counter, which is the most common way a glass pitcher chips, but it will not save it from a fall.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have small children who pour their own water, or anyone in the house with a weak grip, the 7-cup size is a safer choice than the 10-cup. It is lighter, easier to pour with one hand, and more likely to fit the fridge door. A glass pitcher you are nervous to pick up is one you will stop using.
          </p>

