        <div id="cost-per-gallon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Filter Cost per Gallon
          </h2>

          <p>
            The purchase price of a glass pitcher is a one-time cost. The filter is the recurring one, and it is where glass models vary most. The useful number is cost per gallon: the price of a replacement divided by the gallons it is rated for. Divide by the months instead and you will be fooled by filters with short gallon ratings that only last the stated months if you barely use them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">LifeStraw's Two-Stage System</h3>

          <p>
            LifeStraw's two-stage system is the most complicated to price. The membrane microfilter is rated for 264 gallons, about a year, and LifeStraw lists it at $24.95. The carbon and ion-exchange cartridge is rated for 40 gallons, about two months, and LifeStraw sells a six-month carbon pack at $39.95. Its one-year pack, a membrane plus six carbon filters, is listed at $75.95. Six carbon filters cover 240 gallons, so at a gallon a day you will run out before the year is up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Waterdrop's Single Cartridge</h3>

          <p>
            The Waterdrop glass pitchers use a single cartridge rated for about 100 gallons, roughly two months. Fewer changes and one part to buy make the routine simpler. Check the current price of a replacement multi-pack and divide by 100 gallons per filter to compare it with the LifeStraw.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Aarke's Granule Bags</h3>

          <p>
            Aarke prices by the bag of granules rather than by the cartridge. Each bag is rated for up to 120 liters, about 32 gallons, or four weeks. At Aarke's European list price of 20 euros a bag, that works out to roughly 63 euro cents per gallon, before any multi-pack discount. That makes it the most expensive per gallon of the three systems. You are paying for the stainless steel cartridge and the absence of plastic waste.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rated Life and When to Change</h3>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">System</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rated life per replacement</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How to compare</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">LifeStraw Home Glass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Carbon 40 gal / ~2 months; membrane 264 gal / ~1 year</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Carbon pack price / 40 + membrane price / 264</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Waterdrop PT-09W / PT-72W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 100 gal / ~2 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cartridge price / 100</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Aarke Purifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120 L (~32 gal) / 4 weeks per bag</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bag price / 32</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, change the filter on the gallon rating, not on a feeling. A spent carbon filter stops removing chlorine first, which you will taste. It can stop reducing metals well before the taste changes, which you will not. Our guide to <a href="/how-often-to-change-brita-filter/">how often to change a pitcher filter</a> covers the gallon-tracking habit that keeps any of these honest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-glass-wont-fix" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What a Glass Pitcher Will Not Fix
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Pitcher Media Cannot Remove</h3>

          <p>
            No gravity pitcher, glass or plastic, removes everything. Carbon and ion-exchange media are good at chlorine, taste, odor and some metals. They do little for total dissolved solids, nitrates, most fluoride, arsenic or hardness. If a water report shows any of those above a level you care about, a pitcher of any material is the wrong tool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When You Need Reverse Osmosis</h3>

          <p>
            The tool that does handle them is reverse osmosis, which forces water through a membrane fine enough to reject dissolved salts. You cannot do that by gravity in a pitcher; it needs pressure. If you want reverse osmosis with a glass vessel at the end, the countertop units in our <a href="/best-reverse-osmosis-water-filter-pitcher/">reverse osmosis water filter pitcher guide</a> plug in, pump water through a membrane, and several dispense into a glass carafe. They cost several times more than a glass pitcher, and they do a different job.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you would rather not have a box on the counter, an under-sink reverse osmosis or carbon block system does the same work at the tap. Our <a href="/best-under-sink-water-filter/">under sink water filter guide</a> covers both types. You can still fill a glass carafe from the dedicated faucet, which gives you glass storage with stronger filtration than any pitcher.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hard Water</h3>

          <p>
            Hard water is the other common mismatch. A pitcher filter will not stop scale on your kettle or spots on your glasses in any meaningful way, and Aarke's Pure granules are the only ones here that claim to reduce limescale at all. If scale is the problem across the whole house, a softener is the fix, and the <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">whole-home water filtration guide</a> explains where each kind of system fits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Get a Water Report First</h3>

          <p>
            Get a water report before you decide. City water customers can read their utility's annual Consumer Confidence Report for free. Well owners need a lab test. Either one tells you whether a pitcher is enough or whether you are about to spend money on the wrong kind of filter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Cleaning and Living With Glass
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Glass Cleans Easier</h3>

          <p>
            One of the practical advantages of glass is cleaning. Plastic pitchers develop a film over time that is hard to scrub out of corners and scratches. Glass rinses clean, does not hold odor, and shows you immediately when it needs washing. Check each maker's instructions before you load the dishwasher, though, because they differ.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Dishwasher Guidance</h3>

          <p>
            LifeStraw's own page is not fully consistent on this. Its FAQ says the glass pitcher is dishwasher safe and the lid is hand wash only, while its spec sheet says to hand wash the pitcher and filter housing with the filters removed. The safe reading is to hand wash the housing and lid and treat the glass as dishwasher-tolerant on a gentle cycle. Aarke says its whole filter system is dishwasher safe at 60 C once you remove the granules.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rinse, Flush and Keep the Part Number</h3>

          <p>
            Rinse the reservoir and lid every time you change a filter. That is where biofilm tends to start, because it holds unfiltered water at room temperature in a dark space. Keep the pitcher in the fridge rather than on the counter. Chlorine is the reason tap water does not grow much in the pipes, and you have just filtered it out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pre-soak or flush new cartridges exactly as the maker says. Carbon filters release fine black dust on their first few fills, which is harmless but looks alarming in a clear glass pitcher. It shows up more in glass than in tinted plastic, so run the first fill or two through and pour them on a plant.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep the original box or note the replacement part number somewhere. Glass pitchers from smaller brands use proprietary cartridges, and finding the exact replacement two years from now is easier if you wrote it down today.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip a Glass Pitcher
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heavy Water Users</h3>

          <p>
            A glass filter pitcher is not the right buy for everyone, and we would rather say so here than in a return request. If your household drinks more than about a gallon of filtered water a day, a pitcher of any material becomes a chore. You will be refilling it three or four times a day, and the filters will run out on a monthly rather than bimonthly schedule. A countertop dispenser or a filter at the tap is the better fit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Contaminants Not on the Label</h3>

          <p>
            If your water has a specific health contaminant that is not on a pitcher's certification list, glass will not help. That includes lead for the Waterdrop and Aarke models, and fluoride, nitrate or arsenic for all of them. Buy the filter that is certified for your problem first, and accept a plastic jug if that is what it comes in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Renters and Young Kids</h3>

          <p>
            If you rent, move often, or have young kids who pour their own drinks, think hard about breakage. A plastic pitcher with a lead-certified filter is a better health outcome than a glass pitcher you end up not using because you are worried it will break.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Strict Plastic-Free Goals</h3>

          <p>
            And if your only goal is to avoid plastic touching your water, know that a gravity filter pitcher cannot fully deliver that. The Aarke gets closest, at the cost of any published contaminant-reduction certification. The LifeStraw gets you glass storage plus documented lead and microplastics claims, at the cost of a plastic cartridge. That is the real trade-off in this category, and no listing will make it go away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are glass water filter pitchers better than plastic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are better at not holding odors, not scratching, surviving the dishwasher and keeping filtered water out of long contact with plastic. They are not automatically better at filtering. The filter cartridge decides that, and on most glass pitchers it is still housed in plastic. Choose the certification you need first, then choose glass if you want it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a completely plastic-free water filter pitcher?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not among the gravity pitchers covered here. The Aarke Purifier comes closest, with a glass carafe and a stainless steel cartridge filled with loose granules, but Aarke still lists food-grade thermoplastic and silicone in its materials. Every other glass pitcher here uses a plastic cartridge housing, reservoir and lid.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which glass filter pitcher removes lead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Of the pitchers in this guide, only LifeStraw publishes lead reduction for its glass Home pitcher, listing WQA certification to NSF/ANSI 53 for lead, mercury, cadmium, copper and cysts. Waterdrop's glass pitcher listings cite NSF/ANSI 53 but do not list lead among the reductions, and Aarke lists no NSF contaminant certification.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do glass filter pitchers remove microplastics?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                LifeStraw lists WQA certification to NSF/ANSI 401 for microplastics on its glass Home pitcher, the only one here with that claim. The glass body also stops the pitcher itself from adding to the problem during storage. For the broadest reduction of dissolved contaminants, a countertop or under-sink reverse osmosis system goes further than any pitcher.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How heavy is a full glass water filter pitcher?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Water alone weighs about 3.6 pounds in a 7-cup pitcher and 5.2 pounds in a 10-cup one. Add the empty jug, about 2.25 pounds for the LifeStraw by its maker's figure, and a full glass pitcher lands in the 6 to 8 pound range. That is noticeably heavier than plastic, especially on a refrigerator door shelf.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a glass water filter pitcher in the dishwasher?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the model. Aarke says its whole filter system is dishwasher safe at 60 C once the granules are removed. LifeStraw's FAQ says its glass pitcher is dishwasher safe and the lid is hand wash only, while its spec sheet says to hand wash. Always remove the filter cartridge before washing anything.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do you change the filter in a glass pitcher?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Go by the gallon rating. LifeStraw's carbon stage is rated for 40 gallons, about two months, and its membrane for 264 gallons, about a year. Waterdrop's glass pitcher cartridge is rated for about 100 gallons. Aarke's granules last up to 120 liters, about 32 gallons, or four weeks. Heavy use shortens all of these.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Glass and Ignoring the Filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The glass body is the part you see, but the cartridge decides what comes out of the spout. A beautiful glass pitcher with a taste-only filter does nothing for lead or PFAS. Pick the certification you need from the maker's published list first, then choose glass among the models that meet it, not the other way round.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading NSF/ANSI 372 as a Lead Claim</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                372 certifies that the product's materials are low-lead. It does not say the filter removes lead from your water. When a glass pitcher listing shows 42, 53 and 372 together, the contaminant list for 53 is what tells you about lead. On the Waterdrop glass pitchers, lead is not on it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting Plastic-Free From a Filter Pitcher</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most glass pitchers still use a plastic cartridge housing, a plastic reservoir and a plastic lid. Glass changes where filtered water sits for hours, which is a real reduction in contact time. It does not eliminate contact. Only the Aarke uses a stainless steel cartridge, and it still lists some thermoplastic parts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overloading the Fridge Door</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A full 10-cup glass pitcher weighs roughly 7 to 8 pounds and swings every time the door opens. Many door bins are not built for that. If the shelf flexes, move the pitcher to a main shelf or buy the 7-cup size. A broken pitcher and a cracked door bin cost more than the size difference.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Changing Filters by the Calendar Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gallon ratings are the real limit. LifeStraw's carbon stage is rated for 40 gallons, Waterdrop's cartridge for about 100, and an Aarke bag for about 32. A heavy-use household can blow through those well before the month count. Track gallons, or at least refills, so a spent filter does not quietly stop working.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

