        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy a glass water filter pitcher to keep filtered water out of long contact with plastic and to own a jug that stays clear, odor-free and presentable. Do not buy one expecting the glass to improve the filtration. For most households the LifeStraw Home glass pitcher is the right call, because it pairs glass storage with published WQA certification for lead and microplastics.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If the goal is the least plastic possible and your water only needs a taste and chlorine fix, the Aarke Purifier is the better buy, with the honest caveat that it publishes no contaminant-reduction certification. If you need dissolved solids, fluoride or nitrate removed, skip pitchers entirely and look at countertop or under-sink reverse osmosis.
          </p>
